= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the basis of <1/b/solution> for the two doublets, treating the barred left-handed fields as the components to which the printed <representation> assignment applies. Write $x=(\bar\psi_L^1,\bar\psi_L^2)^T$ and $y=(\psi_R^1,\psi_R^2)^T$. The doublet contribution to the <Yukawa interaction> is $H x^T L y$, so <invariant tensors> obey
$$
D_1(Q)^TLD_1(Q)=L,\qquad P^TLP=L.
$$
The first equation forces $L_{11}=L_{22}=0$, since their factors are $\alpha^{\pm2}\ne1$. The second sets $L_{12}=L_{21}$. Thus the doublet <invariant tensor> is $x_1y_2+x_2y_1$. The product of either doublet with $R_1$ has no <trivial representation>, whereas $R_1\otimes R_1=R_0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\lambda^{\mathrm{(i)}}=
\begin{pmatrix}0&y&0\\y&0&0\\0&0&y_3\end{pmatrix},\qquad y,y_3\in\mathbb C.}
$$
If instead the unbarred left-handed doublet is chosen to transform by $D_1$, its barred components transform by $D_1^*$; in that equivalent basis the upper block is $yI_2$. Exchanging the two left components relates these conventions. The <singular value decomposition> gives the same physical masses in either basis.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Now the doublet <invariant tensor> would satisfy $D_1(Q)^T L D_2(Q)=L$. Its four entries acquire factors $\alpha^3$, $\alpha^{-1}$, $\alpha$, and $\alpha^{-3}$, respectively. None equals $1$, so $L=0$. Equivalently, $R_2\otimes R_3=R_2\oplus R_3$ has no <trivial representation>. The mixed doublet and third-family terms are again forbidden because their <tensor product of group representations> contain no <trivial representation>. Only the product of the two $R_1$ fields survives:
$$
\boxed{\lambda^{\mathrm{(ii)}}=
\begin{pmatrix}0&0&0\\0&0&0\\0&0&y_3\end{pmatrix},\qquad y_3\in\mathbb C.}
$$

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Let $v_H=\langle H\rangle$ be the <vacuum expectation value> in the convention $M=v_H\lambda$. For canonically normalized <fermion> kinetic terms, physical <charged lepton> masses are the nonnegative values in the <singular value decomposition> of $M$, equivalently the square roots of the <eigenvalues> of $M^\dagger M$.

In case (i),
$$
M^\dagger M=|v_H|^2\operatorname{diag}(|y|^2,|y|^2,|y_3|^2),\qquad
\boxed{\{m_1,m_2,m_3\}=|v_H|\{|y|,|y|,|y_3|\}.}
$$
The opposite algebraic <eigenvalues> $y$ and $-y$ of the doublet block do not represent different physical masses. The unavoidable twofold degeneracy conflicts with the three distinct nonzero measured <charged lepton> masses, for any assignment of the three families.

In case (ii),
$$
\boxed{\{m_1,m_2,m_3\}=\{0,0,|v_Hy_3|\}.}
$$
One can fit the <tau lepton> mass by choosing $|v_Hy_3|$, but the <electron> and <muon> remain massless. Thus neither exact <dihedral group> assignment with this single singlet <complex scalar field> reproduces the observed spectrum. Additional symmetry-breaking <Yukawa interactions> or other sources of mass are required; a basis change cannot remove the degeneracy or change the <matrix rank>.

