= Solution
{parent=a}

Normalize the <simple roots> to $(\alpha_i,\alpha_i)=2$. The <A2 root system> has $(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)=-1$, so its <Dynkin diagram> is two nodes joined by one bond:
$$
\underset{\alpha_1}{\circ}\! -\!\underset{\alpha_2}{\circ},\qquad
\boxed{A=\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\-1&2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The <fundamental weights> satisfy $(\omega_i,\alpha_j)=\delta_{ij}$. Hence the <Gram matrix> in the <fundamental weight> basis, the metric for <Dynkin label> coordinates, is
$$
\boxed{G_{ij}=(\omega_i,\omega_j)=(A^{-1})_{ij}
=\frac13\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
For <weights of a representation> with <Dynkin labels> $a$ and $b$, their <inner product> is $a^TGb$. In the <simple root> basis the metric is instead $A$ itself; stating the basis removes this apparent convention ambiguity.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $a=a_1$, $b=a_2$ with $a,b\geq0$, and let $\lambda=a\omega_1+b\omega_2$ be the <highest weight>. The <positive roots> are $\alpha_1$, $\alpha_2$ and $\alpha_1+\alpha_2$, and the <Weyl vector> is $\rho=\omega_1+\omega_2$. The <Weyl dimension formula> gives
$$
d(a,b)=\prod_{\alpha>0}\frac{(\lambda+\rho,\alpha)}{(\rho,\alpha)}
=\frac{a+1}{1}\frac{b+1}{1}\frac{a+b+2}{2},\qquad
\boxed{d(a,b)=\tfrac12(a+1)(b+1)(a+b+2).}
$$
For the <quadratic Casimir operator>, use the root-length normalization $C(\lambda)=(\lambda,\lambda+2\rho)$. Substituting the <Gram matrix> in <2/a/solution> yields
$$
\boxed{C(a,b)=\frac23\bigl(a^2+ab+b^2+3a+3b\bigr).}
$$
Taking the <trace> of the defining <quadratic Casimir operator> identity gives $d(a,b)C(a,b)=\dim(\mathfrak{su}(3))c(a,b)=8c(a,b)$, where $c$ is the <Dynkin index> in the matching normalization. Therefore
$$
\boxed{c(a,b)=\frac{(a+1)(b+1)(a+b+2)}{24}
\bigl(a^2+ab+b^2+3a+3b\bigr).}
$$
In particular $c(\mathbf3)=1$ and $c(\mathbf8)=6$. The common particle-physics convention uses $C_2=C/2$ and $T=c/2$, giving $C_2(\mathbf3)=4/3$ and $T(\mathbf3)=1/2$. The subsequent discrete <gauge anomaly> congruences use the integer <Dynkin index> $c$, while the sign test for the supplied <beta function (physics)> is independent of a common rescaling of all indices.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <Weyl dimension formula> is increasing in each nonnegative <Dynkin label>. If $a\geq3$ or $b\geq3$, the dimension is at least $d(3,0)=10$. Among $0\leq a,b\leq2$, the cases with both labels nonzero, other than $(1,1)$, have dimension at least $d(2,1)=15$. Thus the following is the complete list of <irreducible representations> of dimension strictly less than $10$:
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|r|r|r}
(a,b)&R&d&C&c\\\hline
(0,0)&\mathbf1&1&0&0\\
(1,0)&\mathbf3&3&8/3&1\\
(0,1)&\overline{\mathbf3}&3&8/3&1\\
(2,0)&\mathbf6&6&20/3&5\\
(0,2)&\overline{\mathbf6}&6&20/3&5\\
(1,1)&\mathbf8&8&6&6
\end{array}}
$$
Here $\mathbf6=\operatorname{Sym}^2\mathbf3$ is the <two-index symmetric representation> and $\mathbf8$ is the <adjoint representation>. Interchanging $a$ and $b$ gives the <conjugate representation>, explaining equal dimensions, <Casimir eigenvalues> and <Dynkin indices> in each conjugate pair. In the alternative convention of <2/b/solution>, divide the $C$ and $c$ columns by two.

