= Solution
{parent=i}

The <Dynkin index> is additive over a <direct sum of group representations>, so $n$ copies of the <fundamental representation> have $c(r_S)=n$. With $c(\mathrm{ad})=6$, the supplied <beta function (physics)> becomes
$$
\beta(g)=-\frac{g^3}{192\pi^2}(132-n).
$$
For positive <gauge coupling> $g$, <asymptotic freedom> at this order requires $\beta(g)<0$, hence $n<132$. For nonnegative integer $n$,
$$
\boxed{n_{\max}=131.}
$$
At $n=132$ the one-loop coefficient vanishes, so this formula alone does not establish <asymptotic freedom>. This count follows the scalar multiplicity convention of the printed <beta function (physics)>; changing how a real or complex <scalar field> is counted requires changing its coefficient too.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <two-index symmetric representation> of <SU(3)> is $\mathbf6$, with <Dynkin index> $5$. Thus $n$ copies have $c(r_S)=5n$, and the supplied <beta function (physics)> is
$$
\beta(g)=-\frac{g^3}{192\pi^2}(132-5n).
$$
The strict sign condition for <asymptotic freedom> is $n<132/5=26.4$, giving
$$
\boxed{n_{\max}=26.}
$$
Indeed $132-5(26)=2>0$, while $132-5(27)=-3<0$.

