= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the integer normalization $c(\mathbf3)=1$ from <2/b/solution>. For two <fundamental representations>, the specified discrete <gauge anomaly> coefficient is $\mathcal A=q_1+q_2$. Consequently all solutions in the <finite cyclic group> of charges are
$$
\boxed{(q_1,q_2)=(q,-q)\pmod n,\qquad q\in\mathbb Z_n.}
$$
There are $n$ ordered charge pairs. The trivial pair is included: the problem does not require a faithful action. If a faithful action on these <Weyl fermions> were additionally required, one would retain precisely the $q$ coprime to $n$.

This cancels the discrete <gauge anomaly> defined in the paper. Two left-handed <fundamental representations> by themselves have a nonzero pure cubic <SU(3)> <gauge anomaly>; a complete <gauge theory> would need additional <fermions> to cancel it. That separate condition does not alter the requested discrete congruence.

= ii
{parent=e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the <fundamental representation> and the <two-index symmetric representation>, the <Dynkin indices> are $1$ and $5$. Therefore the specified discrete <gauge anomaly> condition for $\mathbb Z_4$ is
$$
q_1+5q_2\equiv q_1+q_2\equiv0\pmod4.
$$
All ordered solutions are
$$
\boxed{(q_1,q_2)\in\{(0,0),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\}\pmod4.}
$$
Only $(1,3)$ and $(3,1)$ give a faithful $\mathbb Z_4$ action on this pair of <Weyl fermions>; faithfulness was not imposed. As in <2/e/i/solution>, cancellation here concerns the stipulated discrete <gauge anomaly>, not cancellation of the pure cubic <SU(3)> <gauge anomaly>. The integer normalization matters: one must not replace $c$ by a half-integer trace index inside an unchanged modular congruence.

= 3
{parent=2022}
