= Solution
{parent=c}

Write the defining space as $V=A\oplus B$, where $A\cong\mathbb C^2$ and $B\cong\mathbb C^3$. Use ordered pairs for the <SU(2)> and <SU(3)> factors, in that order. The generator $X_c$ in <3/b/solution> gives <U(1) charges> $3$ on $A$ and $-2$ on $B$, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbf5\downarrow=(\mathbf2,\mathbf1)_3\oplus(\mathbf1,\mathbf3)_{-2}.}
$$
Taking the <conjugate representation> reverses the <U(1) charges>. The <SU(2)> doublet is equivalent to its <dual representation>, while the <SU(3)> triplet becomes the antitriplet:
$$
\boxed{\overline{\mathbf5}\downarrow=(\mathbf2,\mathbf1)_{-3}\oplus(\mathbf1,\overline{\mathbf3})_2.}
$$

For the <two-index antisymmetric representation>, split the <antisymmetric tensor> $T^{IJ}=-T^{JI}$ into $T^{ab}$, $T^{a\mu}$ and $T^{\mu\nu}$, with $a,b=1,2$ and $\mu,\nu=3,4,5$. The <exterior square of a direct sum> gives
$$
\Lambda^2(A\oplus B)=\Lambda^2A\oplus(A\otimes B)\oplus\Lambda^2B.
$$
The first term is an <SU(2)> singlet, the middle is $(\mathbf2,\mathbf3)$, and $\Lambda^2\mathbf3\cong\overline{\mathbf3}$ by contraction with the invariant alternating <tensor>. Adding the <U(1) charges> of the two indices gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbf{10}\downarrow=(\mathbf1,\mathbf1)_6\oplus(\mathbf2,\mathbf3)_1\oplus(\mathbf1,\overline{\mathbf3})_{-4}.}
$$
The dimensions $1+6+3=10$ check the <branching of a representation>. In the convention $Q_{\rm em}=T_3+Y$, <weak hypercharge> is $Y=X_c/6$.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <complexification of a Lie algebra> in the <adjoint representation> of <SU(5)> consists of traceless endomorphisms of $V=A\oplus B$. Splitting an endomorphism into its four blocks gives traceless endomorphisms of $A$ and $B$, one relative block-scalar direction, and the two off-diagonal spaces $\operatorname{Hom}(B,A)$ and $\operatorname{Hom}(A,B)$.

The first three pieces transform as $(\mathbf3,\mathbf1)_0$, $(\mathbf1,\mathbf8)_0$ and $(\mathbf1,\mathbf1)_0$. The off-diagonal pieces have <U(1) charges> equal to output minus input charge, namely $3-(-2)=5$ and $-2-3=-5$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbf{24}\downarrow=(\mathbf3,\mathbf1)_0\oplus(\mathbf1,\mathbf8)_0\oplus(\mathbf1,\mathbf1)_0
\oplus(\mathbf2,\overline{\mathbf3})_5\oplus(\mathbf2,\mathbf3)_{-5}.}
$$
The dimensions are $3+8+1+6+6=24$. The first three summands are precisely the <gauge fields> of the local <gauge group> $H\times U_c(1)$. The extra <gauge fields> of the <SU(5)> <gauge theory> transform as
$$
\boxed{(\mathbf2,\overline{\mathbf3})_5\oplus(\mathbf2,\mathbf3)_{-5}.}
$$
Hermiticity relates the two off-diagonal blocks, so these describe twelve real <gauge fields>, or six complex fields and their conjugates. Globally, the subgroup generated by $H$ and $U_c(1)$ inside <SU(5)> is $(SU(2)\times SU(3)\times U(1))/\mathbb Z_6$, since the parametrization $(U_2,U_3,e^{i\theta})\mapsto\operatorname{diag}(e^{3i\theta}U_2,e^{-2i\theta}U_3)$ has a six-element <kernel>. This finite quotient does not alter the stated <branching of a representation>.

= e
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The <symmetric square of a direct sum> gives
$$
\operatorname{Sym}^2(A\oplus B)=\operatorname{Sym}^2A\oplus(A\otimes B)\oplus\operatorname{Sym}^2B.
$$
Now $\operatorname{Sym}^2\mathbf2=\mathbf3$ and $\operatorname{Sym}^2\mathbf3=\mathbf6$, rather than the singlet and antitriplet arising from <exterior squares>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbf{15}\downarrow=(\mathbf3,\mathbf1)_6\oplus(\mathbf2,\mathbf3)_1\oplus(\mathbf1,\mathbf6)_{-4}.}
$$
The dimensions are $3+6+6=15$.

By contrast, <3/c/solution> gives the following left-handed <Weyl fermion> content of $\overline{\mathbf5}\oplus\mathbf{10}$:
$$
L:(\mathbf2,\mathbf1)_{-3},\quad d^c:(\mathbf1,\overline{\mathbf3})_2,\quad
Q:(\mathbf2,\mathbf3)_1,\quad u^c:(\mathbf1,\overline{\mathbf3})_{-4},\quad
e^c:(\mathbf1,\mathbf1)_6.
$$
These are one <Standard Model> family, with <weak hypercharge> $Y=q_c/6$. The $\mathbf{15}$ contains $Q$ but lacks $L$, $d^c$, $u^c$ and $e^c$; instead it contains an <SU(2)> triplet and an <SU(3)> sextet. Equality of total dimensions therefore does not give the same <representation> content, and $\mathbf{15}$ cannot supply the family.

= 4
{parent=2022}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <A5 root system> with <simple roots> $\alpha_i=e_i-e_{i+1}$ for $1\leq i\leq5$, in the hyperplane $\sum_{i=1}^6x_i=0$. The <Dynkin diagram> is the five-node chain
$$
\underset{1}{\circ}\!-\!\underset{2}{\circ}\!-\!\underset{3}{\circ}\!-\!\underset{4}{\circ}\!-\!\underset{5}{\circ}.
$$
Its <Cartan matrix> is
$$
\boxed{A_5=\begin{pmatrix}
2&-1&0&0&0\\-1&2&-1&0&0\\0&-1&2&-1&0\\0&0&-1&2&-1\\0&0&0&-1&2
\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Deleting the final node leaves the <A4 root system>, with <simple roots> $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_4$. Concretely, the <Lie algebra homomorphism> $\mathfrak{sl}_5(\mathbb C)\hookrightarrow\mathfrak{sl}_6(\mathbb C)$ sends $X$ to $\operatorname{diag}(X,0)$ and preserves the <Lie bracket>. This realizes the required <Lie subalgebra> and fixes the embedding used below.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the <special unitary group> $SU(N)$, denote a one-column <Young tableau> with $k$ empty boxes by $\mathcal C_k$. Explicitly the columns needed here are
$$
\mathcal C_1=\boxed{\phantom{a}},\quad
\mathcal C_2=\begin{array}{|c|}\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\end{array},\quad
\mathcal C_3=\begin{array}{|c|}\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\end{array},\quad
\mathcal C_4=\begin{array}{|c|}\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\end{array},\quad
\mathcal C_5=\begin{array}{|c|}\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\phantom{a}\\\hline\end{array}.
$$
A column $\mathcal C_k$ is the <exterior power> $\Lambda^kV$, with <highest weight> $\omega_k$ and dimension $\binom Nk$. Contraction with the invariant volume <tensor> identifies $V^*\cong\Lambda^{N-1}V$ and $\Lambda^2V^*\cong\Lambda^{N-2}V$. This gives all the requested <Dynkin labels>, <Young tableaux>, dimensions and <tensors>:
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}
\text{algebra}&R&\text{highest label}&\text{tableau}&\text{dimension and tensor}\\\hline
A_5&\mathbf6&(1,0,0,0,0)&\mathcal C_1&6:\ v^I\\
A_5&\overline{\mathbf6}&(0,0,0,0,1)&\mathcal C_5&6:\ v_I\\
A_5&\mathbf{15}&(0,1,0,0,0)&\mathcal C_2&15:\ T^{IJ}=-T^{JI}\\
A_5&\overline{\mathbf{15}}&(0,0,0,1,0)&\mathcal C_4&15:\ T_{IJ}=-T_{JI}\\\hline
A_4&\mathbf5&(1,0,0,0)&\mathcal C_1&5:\ v^i\\
A_4&\overline{\mathbf5}&(0,0,0,1)&\mathcal C_4&5:\ v_i\\
A_4&\mathbf{10}&(0,1,0,0)&\mathcal C_2&10:\ T^{ij}=-T^{ji}\\
A_4&\overline{\mathbf{10}}&(0,0,1,0)&\mathcal C_3&10:\ T_{ij}=-T_{ji}
\end{array}}
$$
Here $1\leq I,J\leq6$ and $1\leq i,j\leq5$. Lower indices denote the <dual representation>, equivalent to the <conjugate representation> for these <compact groups>. The <SU(6)> $\mathbf{15}$ in this question is an <exterior square>; the <SU(5)> $\mathbf{15}$ in <3/e/solution> is a <symmetric square>. The ambient <group> is essential to distinguish them.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the <special unitary group> $SU(N)$ define $\varepsilon_i=e_i-\frac1N\sum_{j=1}^Ne_j$. These are the <weights of a representation> in the defining space, with $\sum_i\varepsilon_i=0$. Their <Dynkin labels> are
$$
(w_i)_r=(\varepsilon_i,\alpha_r)=\delta_{ir}-\delta_{i,r+1},\qquad1\leq r<N.
$$
The <dual representation> negates each <weight of a representation>, and the <tensor product of group representations> adds them. Consequently the complete <weight systems> are
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal W(V)&=\{\varepsilon_i:1\leq i\leq N\},&
\mathcal W(V^*)&=\{-\varepsilon_i:1\leq i\leq N\},\\
\mathcal W(\Lambda^2V)&=\{\varepsilon_i+\varepsilon_j:1\leq i<j\leq N\},&
\mathcal W(\Lambda^2V^*)&=\{-\varepsilon_i-\varepsilon_j:1\leq i<j\leq N\}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
Every listed <weight of a representation> has multiplicity one: the corresponding <weight basis> vectors are $v_i$, $v^i$, $v_i\wedge v_j$ and $v^i\wedge v^j$. Distinct pairs give distinct projected sums for $N=5,6$. For an explicit enumeration in <Dynkin labels>, the two defining <weight systems> are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
i&A_5:\mathbf6&A_4:\mathbf5\\\hline
1&(1,0,0,0,0)&(1,0,0,0)\\
2&(-1,1,0,0,0)&(-1,1,0,0)\\
3&(0,-1,1,0,0)&(0,-1,1,0)\\
4&(0,0,-1,1,0)&(0,0,-1,1)\\
5&(0,0,0,-1,1)&(0,0,0,-1)\\
6&(0,0,0,0,-1)&\text{not present}
\end{array}
$$
The <weight systems> for $\overline{\mathbf6}$ and $\overline{\mathbf5}$ are the negatives of the entries in their respective columns, without reversing coordinate order.

For the <exterior squares>, the full enumerations are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
ij&A_5:\mathbf{15}&A_4:\mathbf{10}\\\hline
12&(0,1,0,0,0)&(0,1,0,0)\\
13&(1,-1,1,0,0)&(1,-1,1,0)\\
14&(1,0,-1,1,0)&(1,0,-1,1)\\
15&(1,0,0,-1,1)&(1,0,0,-1)\\
16&(1,0,0,0,-1)&\text{not present}\\
23&(-1,0,1,0,0)&(-1,0,1,0)\\
24&(-1,1,-1,1,0)&(-1,1,-1,1)\\
25&(-1,1,0,-1,1)&(-1,1,0,-1)\\
26&(-1,1,0,0,-1)&\text{not present}\\
34&(0,-1,0,1,0)&(0,-1,0,1)\\
35&(0,-1,1,-1,1)&(0,-1,1,-1)\\
36&(0,-1,1,0,-1)&\text{not present}\\
45&(0,0,-1,0,1)&(0,0,-1,0)\\
46&(0,0,-1,1,-1)&\text{not present}\\
56&(0,0,0,-1,0)&\text{not present}
\end{array}
$$
The negatives of the $A_5$ column are exactly the fifteen <weights of a representation> of $\overline{\mathbf{15}}$; the negatives of the $A_4$ column are exactly the ten of $\overline{\mathbf{10}}$. Thus the tables specify all eight <weight systems>, including multiplicities, rather than only their <highest weights>.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For the embedding in <4/a/solution>, restriction retains the first four <Dynkin label> coordinates, since the <simple coroots> of $A_4$ are the first four of $A_5$. In a coordinate-free version of the same projection, let $\eta_i$ be the five defining <weights of a representation> of $A_4$. Restriction gives $\varepsilon_i\mapsto\eta_i$ for $1\leq i\leq5$, while $\varepsilon_6\mapsto0$.

For the <fundamental representation>, its first five <weights of a representation> therefore form a $\mathbf5$ and the sixth is the zero <weight of a representation> of a <trivial representation>. Negating the <weights of a representation> gives the <conjugate representation>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf6\downarrow_{SU(5)}=\mathbf5\oplus\mathbf1,\qquad
\overline{\mathbf6}\downarrow_{SU(5)}=\overline{\mathbf5}\oplus\mathbf1.}
$$
For the <two-index antisymmetric representation>, split the fifteen <weights of a representation> into the ten with $i<j\leq5$ and the five with $j=6$. Their restricted values are $\eta_i+\eta_j$ and $\eta_i$, respectively. These are precisely the <weight systems> of $\mathbf{10}$ and $\mathbf5$. Negating again gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbf{15}\downarrow_{SU(5)}=\mathbf{10}\oplus\mathbf5,\qquad
\overline{\mathbf{15}}\downarrow_{SU(5)}=\overline{\mathbf{10}}\oplus\overline{\mathbf5}.}
$$
For instance the highest $A_5$ <weight of a representation> $(0,1,0,0,0)$ restricts to $(0,1,0,0)$ for the $\mathbf{10}$, while $(1,0,0,0,-1)$ restricts to $(1,0,0,0)$ for the $\mathbf5$. The restricted <weight systems> are disjoint unions of multiplicity-one sets in these cases, and their dimensions check as $6=5+1$ and $15=10+5$.

= e
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

One left-handed <Standard Model> family requires the <SU(5)> <representation> content $\mathbf{10}\oplus\overline{\mathbf5}$. No single <irreducible representation> in <4/b/solution> contains both. At the level of containing the required fields, the smallest choice is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf{15}\oplus\overline{\mathbf6}\downarrow
=(\mathbf{10}\oplus\overline{\mathbf5})\oplus\mathbf5\oplus\mathbf1.}
$$
The additional <SU(5)> multiplets are a $\mathbf5$ and a <trivial representation>. Another minimal choice using only two of the listed <irreducible representations> is
$$
\mathbf{15}\oplus\overline{\mathbf{15}}\downarrow
=(\mathbf{10}\oplus\overline{\mathbf5})\oplus(\overline{\mathbf{10}}\oplus\mathbf5).
$$
Its additional multiplets form the <conjugate representation> of a family. More generally, for multiplicities $(m_6,m_{\bar6},m_{15},m_{\overline{15}})$, <branching of a representation> gives
$$
(m_6+m_{15})\mathbf5\oplus(m_{\bar6}+m_{\overline{15}})\overline{\mathbf5}
\oplus m_{15}\mathbf{10}\oplus m_{\overline{15}}\overline{\mathbf{10}}
\oplus(m_6+m_{\bar6})\mathbf1.
$$
Thus containing a family requires $m_{15}\geq1$ and $m_{\bar6}+m_{\overline{15}}\geq1$.

For a consistent chiral <grand unified theory>, containment alone is insufficient. Normalize the cubic <gauge anomaly> coefficient by $A(\mathbf6)=1$. For the <special unitary group> $SU(N)$, $A(\Lambda^2\mathbf N)=N-4$, so for <SU(6)> it is $2$. To verify this directly, take a traceless diagonal generator with entries $x_i$: its <exterior square> has entries $x_i+x_j$, and
$$
\sum_{i<j}(x_i+x_j)^3=(N-1)\sum_i x_i^3+3\sum_{i\ne j}x_i^2x_j
=(N-4)\sum_i x_i^3.
$$
The <conjugate representation> reverses this cubic <gauge anomaly>. The combination $\mathbf{15}\oplus\overline{\mathbf6}$ has coefficient $2-1=1$, so cannot alone be a quantum-consistent chiral <SU(6)> <gauge theory>. The minimal anomaly-free choice with one net <chiral gauge spectrum> of a family is instead
$$
\boxed{\mathbf{15}\oplus2\overline{\mathbf6}\downarrow
=(\mathbf{10}\oplus\overline{\mathbf5})\oplus(\mathbf5\oplus\overline{\mathbf5})\oplus2\mathbf1.}
$$
The additional nonsinglet fields form a <vectorlike gauge spectrum> that can acquire an <SU(5)>-invariant mass, and the two <gauge singlets> may serve as sterile <neutrinos>. More generally, precisely one net <chiral gauge spectrum> of a family with vectorlike extra nonsinglets requires $m_{15}-m_{\overline{15}}=1$ and $m_{\bar6}-m_6=2$; these conditions also cancel the cubic <SU(6)> <gauge anomaly>. The $\mathbf{15}\oplus\overline{\mathbf{15}}$ choice above is anomaly-free but has zero net <chirality>.
